WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.415
From a passing navigator to a community of seven families.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.852
The Portuguese navigator Tristão da Cunha sighted the island in 1506.

00:00:09.852 --> 00:00:16.158
Britain garrisoned it in 1816, officially to stop it being used to rescue Napoleon from

00:00:16.158 --> 00:00:16.883
St Helena.

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:21.885
When the soldiers left, Corporal William Glass and his family stayed.

00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:24.494
Women from St Helena joined in 1827,

00:00:24.494 --> 00:00:30.438
and two Italian sailors shipwrecked in 1892 added the names Repetto and Lavarello.

00:00:31.538 --> 00:00:35.070
On the tenth of October 1961, after weeks of earthquakes,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.664
a vent opened beside the village and built a dome of lava.

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:43.682
The whole population was evacuated, first to Nightingale Island, then to England.

00:00:44.782 --> 00:00:47.207
They lived near Southampton for two years.

00:00:47.207 --> 00:00:51.420
Although they were offered permanent resettlement, most voted to go home,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.632
and returned in 1963.

00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:56.269
Journalists turned the return into a parable about modern life.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:01:01.002
The islanders' own accounts stress their land, their boats and their independence.
